"Brings multiple perspectives, relationships, tensions and contextual features of a messy situation into an exploratory visual account.\n\nUse when: Investigate an unclear situation before deciding what the problem is. Help participants discuss different interpretations of a shared context.\n\nAvoid when: The audience needs formal notation, quantitative prediction or an agreed process specification.\n\nVisual grammar: People, activities and contextual elements use simple recognizable sketches. Brief speech or thought notes show concerns and viewpoints. Relationship marks are explained locally rather than claiming a universal formal grammar.\n\nBuild: Define the situation and invite perspectives from affected participants. Sketch its people, structures, processes and tensions together. Compare interpretations and split complex viewpoints into linked views when needed.\n\nCheck: The picture acknowledges whose perspective it represents. Emotions, assumptions and observed features remain distinguishable. Important conflicting views are preserved instead of forced into consensus.\n\nPitfalls: Decorating a predetermined solution while calling it exploration. Presenting one participant's interpretation as objective completeness.\n\nExample: A market redevelopment picture includes traders, shoppers, deliveries, access concerns and council goals, exposing disagreements before requirements are fixed.\n\nAlternatives: Stakeholder onion map, Causal-loop diagram."
